From: Prabhsimran Singh <prabhsimrans@nvidia.com>
Subject: ggml: ragged Mamba2 and KQ mask ops for packed multi-sequence batches

Ops for serving many sequences of different lengths in one ubatch of a hybrid
(Mamba2 + attention) model, with CUDA implementations:

- ggml_ssm_conv_ragged and ggml_ssm_scan_ragged: packed sequences delimited by
  an offsets tensor. Variants write the final scan states straight into the
  recurrent cache (ggml_ssm_scan_to[_indexed]), update the conv states in place
  for one-token decode (_inplace), fuse the conv bias and SiLU, and verify MTP
  drafts without committing them (_verify, ReplaySSM checkpoint rows).
- Chunked SSD for long segments (ggml_ssm_scan_set_chunks; a fused kernel on
  backends with serving.fused). The fused prefill kernel is bounded to three
  128-thread CTAs per SM (its 45 KB of shared memory fits three times): 12 warps
  instead of 8 hide the latency of its sequential 64-token steps.
- ggml_ssm_gate_norm: the Mamba2 output gate (y + x*D) * silu(z) and its
  grouped RMS norm in one pass.
- ggml_build_kq_mask: the causal KQ mask built on the device from key
  positions, sequence-membership words and query (pos, seq) pairs. The _meta
  variant carries only per-sequence cell lists, for flash attention.

The CPU backend reports these unsupported.

Upstream: not planned in this form; ragged Mamba2 batching could be proposed
once the op interface settles.

